The system is complete and verified. All 16 tests pass, the server runs end-to-end, and runtime artifacts are cleaned up.

## What was built

An internal **invoice approval service** for Nordvik Components Oy — a Node.js/Express + SQLite web service with a REST API and a small demo UI.

**Stack:** Node.js (built-in `node:sqlite`, no native build step), Express, Multer for PDF uploads. Password hashing and signed auth tokens use only `node:crypto`. Just two third-party deps (`express`, `multer`).

**How to run** (from the README):
```bash
npm install
npm run seed     # creates 1 submitter + 2 approver demo users
npm start        # http://localhost:3000
npm test         # 16 tests, isolated in-memory DBs
```

**Every requirement is covered and tested:**

| # | Requirement | Implementation |
|---|-------------|----------------|
| 1 | Email/password login, one role each | scrypt hashing; `CHECK` constraint on role |
| 2 | Submitter creates invoice + PDF | `POST /api/invoices`, PDF mimetype validated |
| 3 | submitted → approved/rejected, approver-only | state machine, terminal states locked |
| 4 | No self-approval | `403` when actor == submitter |
| 5 | >€10k needs two distinct approvers | `UNIQUE(invoice_id, approver_id)` + threshold logic |
| 6 | Append-only audit log | writes on every change + `BEFORE UPDATE/DELETE` triggers reject mutation at the DB layer |
| 7 | Contact email is personal data | never in normal responses; dedicated read endpoint audits **every** read; erasure endpoint |
| 8 | Pending list, filterable | approver list filtered by name substring + amount range, excludes own/already-approved |
| 9 | Email on every state change | stub appends JSON to `data/outbox.log` |
| 10 | Tests + README | `node --test` suite + this README |

**Design choices worth noting:** the append-only guarantee is enforced with SQLite triggers (defense in depth, not just "no endpoint exists"); the counterparty email is structurally excluded from `serializeInvoice` so it can only leak through the audited path; and the two-approver rule is enforced by a DB uniqueness constraint rather than application counting alone.

One thing to flag: `POST /api/auth/register` is open (unauthenticated) so seeding and tests can provision users. For a real internal deployment you'd gate it behind an admin role — I noted this inline in `src/app.js`.
